# Invalidation tests
# Format: OPERATOR file1.yaml file2.yaml [new-builder]
# ==  no tiles should be dirty after rendering file2 (zero invalidation)
# !=  at least one tile should be dirty after rendering file2
# new-builder  build file2 with a fresh display list builder, the way a
#              replaced content process would

# Scrolling a sub-picture should not invalidate tiles
== scroll_subpic_1.yaml scroll_subpic_2.yaml

# Moving a rect within a tile requires a composite (and dirty tile)
!= composite_nop_1.yaml composite_nop_2.yaml

# A repaint that moves scroll offset from the async delta into
# external-scroll-offset (shifting every coordinate inside the scroll frame to
# match) is normalized out by the display list builder, so nothing an interning
# key can see changes and the tile must stay valid. Guards the scroll-invariance
# the interned prim uid relies on; scroll_subpic above only covers async
# scrolling, which never re-runs scene building.
== eso_repaint_1.yaml eso_repaint_2.yaml

# Same, for a repaint that bakes the sticky offset into the emitted coordinates
# and reports it as previously-applied-offset.
== sticky_repaint_1.yaml sticky_repaint_2.yaml

# Text under an animated transform is rasterized in local space; when the
# animation ends it must return to the crisp device path. A bound transform only
# counts as animating once it has actually moved (bug 2051166), so the sequence
# moves property 10 (a -> b) before ending it (b -> c). These lines share the
# persistent property state and must run in order.

# a -> b: the bound transform starts moving, flipping the text from the device
# path to local raster. This must invalidate the tile.
!= anim_text_a.yaml anim_text_b.yaml

# Re-rendering the latched-moving frame with an unchanged value must NOT churn
# the tile (the AnimatedRasterSpace dependency is stable frame-to-frame).
== anim_text_b.yaml anim_text_b.yaml

# b -> c: the animation ends (binding removed) at the same position, so only the
# raster space flips local -> device with the transform value unchanged. The
# tile must still invalidate so the text returns to the device path (bug
# 2056306).
!= anim_text_b.yaml anim_text_c.yaml

# The same again, but the second list comes from a fresh display list builder,
# as it would from a replaced content process. Its interning slot numbering
# restarts, so the receiver drops what the first builder interned and applies
# the new list from scratch (DeltaAction::Reset): the re-interned run gets a
# fresh uid and the tile invalidates, as for the rectangle case below.
!= anim_text_b.yaml anim_text_b.yaml new-builder

# Re-rendering an unchanged rectangle from the same builder does not churn the
# tile: the builder keeps its interned entry and the uid with it.
== composite_nop_1.yaml composite_nop_1.yaml

# A display list built by a different builder for the same pipeline restarts
# interning slot numbering, so the receiver drops what the previous builder
# interned and re-applies the new list from scratch (DeltaAction::Reset). The
# content is byte-for-byte the same as the line above, so this asserts the
# takeover itself: the re-interned rectangle gets a fresh uid and the tile
# invalidates. Conservative rather than ideal, and the price of an
# exactly-unique uid - see doc/dl-builder-interning.md.
!= composite_nop_1.yaml composite_nop_1.yaml new-builder

# Same, but with a coordinate off the app-unit grid the display list declares, on
# the axis the scroll offset does *not* move. Normalization must leave that axis
# alone rather than rounding it onto the grid (bug 2059620).
== offgrid_repaint_1.yaml offgrid_repaint_2.yaml

# A pipeline going away and a new one taking its place. Each step retires the
# previous iframe pipeline and introduces a new one; the removal arrives in its
# own transaction with no display list, so nothing rebuilds the scene at that
# point and the receiver has to hold the pipeline's resources until one does.
!= recycle_pipeline_1.yaml recycle_pipeline_2.yaml
!= recycle_pipeline_2.yaml recycle_pipeline_3.yaml
